  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dense fog tonight Downeast, followed by widespread rain \nshowers and chance for thunderstorms throughout the forecast \narea on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFog: Elevated low level moisture continues to linger throughout\nthe forecast area this evening, particularly throughout the\nDowneast region. Patchy fog that currently remains just off-\nshore late this afternoon may return northwards this evening\nwith the sea breeze and increasing southerly flow, pushing\ninland and leading to areas of dense fog through the night\nDowneast tonight. Another Dense Fog Advisory is possible, though\nthe areal extent remains uncertain enough at this time that\nconfidence is too low to issue an advisory at this moment. It is\nunlikely the areas of dense fog will push all the way north\nthrough the state, but trends will be closely monitored as\nhumidity levels increase.\n\nRain and storms: A cold front will cross the area Monday,\nincreasing the pressure gradient and leading to gusty winds and\nmoderate rainfall. Total rainfall amounts could exceed an inch\nin areas where moderate rain continues to train over the same\narea through Monday afternoon. Instability will be limited and\nforecast profiles indicate CAPE will be mostly elevated, but the\nchance for thunderstorms remains due to the dynamic forcing\nderived from the synoptic cold front. Rain should move out of\nthe area through Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the area mid week weakens Thursday as upper\nlevel shortwave energy moves in from the west. Models are\ncurrently showing the potential for some weak low pressure\ndevelopment in the area which may kick off some showers and\nstorms. Long range ensemble guidance shows the potential for at\nleast a few hundred J/kg of instability but is relatively\nuncertain in when/where the best forcing for ascent will be so \nwill need to wait to get better clarity on storm location,\nstrength, and coverage.",
